```{r}
# Parametric Bootstrap
# we assumed that data follows a distribution Beta(1, mle)
n = length(xx)
B = 1000
tau.boot <- rep(NA, B) # Init
for(b in 1:B){
x.boot <- rbeta(n, 1, psi(xx)) # bootstrap samples
tau.boot[b] <- 1/(1 + psi(x.boot))
}
bias = mean(tau.boot) - tau(xx)
bias
se = sd(tau.boot)
se
lower = tau(xx) - z *se
lower
upper = tau(xx) + z *se
upper
```
```{r}
# Non parametric Bootstrap
# we do not assume any probabilistic distribution, we rely on empirical resampling from the observed dataset
for (b in 1:B){
  idx <- sample(1:n, replace = T) # resample data with replacement
  x.boot <- xx[idx]
  tau.boot[b] <- 1/(1 + psi(x.boot))
}
bias = mean(tau.boot) - tau(xx)
bias
se = sd(tau.boot)
se
lower = tau(xx) - z *se
lower
upper = tau(xx) + z *se
upper
```

Since both methods produce similar results, we can claim that assuming a beta distribution do not introduce significant bias into our estimates. Since non parametric provided a lower Standard error, the estimates might be slightly more precise.

